# OgaHosting + GitHub Deployment Guide (FedExUS / FreightFlow)

This guide is tailored to the existing Laravel 12 app in this repo. Follow in order.

## 1) Pre-flight (do first)
- Backup your current live site files + database via cPanel/File Manager/Backup Wizard before touching anything.
- Get from OgaHosting: PHP version (recommend 8.2/8.3), MySQL/MariaDB details, document root (public_html/ or subfolder), SSH access (if available), cron/cPanel Git availability, and SMTP details.
- Note: this app uses Laravel Reverb/WebSockets and Redis in places; see section 3 for shared-hosting compatibility notes.

## 2) Repository preparation
- Never commit secrets. Keep .env out of Git.
- Ensure ackend/composer.lock and ackend/package-lock.json are committed.
- endor/, 
ode_modules/, storage/framework/* caches, ootstrap/cache/*, logs, .env, uploaded photos are gitignored.
- If using GitHub, deploy from a protected branch (e.g. main).

## 3) Upload strategy (choose one)
Pick the option your OgaHosting account supports:

### Option A: cPanel Git Version Control (preferred if available)
1. Create repo on GitHub (private recommended).
2. In cPanel > Git Version Control, clone/pull the repo to a directory OUTSIDE public_html (e.g. ~/repositories/fedexus).
3. Point document root to the Laravel public/ directory: either move contents of public/ to public_html/ or set the domain's document root to .../fedexus/backend/public (if supported). Many shared hosts require document root at public_html.
   - If document root cannot be changed to ackend/public, copy ackend/public/* into public_html/ and adjust public/index.php to point to ../backend/bootstrap/app.php and ../backend/vendor/autoload.php (or the correct paths). Keep all other Laravel files outside public_html.
4. Create .cpanel.yml at repo root if using cPanel Git deploy hooks (paths vary). Only add after confirming paths with host.

### Option B: Manual upload (safe and common)
1. Download repo as ZIP from GitHub.
2. Extract locally. Build assets (section 4) or upload prebuilt ackend/public/build/.
3. Upload everything EXCEPT ackend/.env, ackend/node_modules, local caches to your hosting location outside document root where possible, or follow your host's layout. Ensure public/ contents go to web-accessible directory.

## 4) Frontend assets
Assets are built with Vite. Either:
- Build locally: cd backend && npm ci && npm run build and commit/upload public/build/ (already present), OR
- Let host build (usually not available). With shared hosting, prebuild locally is safest.

## 5) Environment configuration (.env)
Copy ackend/.env.example to ackend/.env on server and set:
- APP_ENV=production
- APP_DEBUG=false
- APP_URL=https://yourdomain.com (no trailing slash)
- APP_KEY - **DO NOT regenerate** if migrating existing site with encrypted data. Copy existing key from current .env.
- Database: DB_CONNECTION=mysql, DB_HOST, DB_PORT, DB_DATABASE, DB_USERNAME, DB_PASSWORD
- Sessions/cache/queue: on many shared hosts use ile/database for cache/session/queue unless Redis is provided. Current app may use Redis/reverb - see compatibility.
- Mail: set SMTP via host or provider.
- Payment keys: set from provider (Flutterwave/etc). Keep in .env only.

**Important:** Do not put Admin panel settings (site title/logo/gateways toggles/bank details) in .env. These are DB-backed in the Admin settings system.

## 6) Database
1. Create MySQL DB + user in cPanel > MySQL Databases.
2. Import schema/data. Options:
   - phpMyAdmin import of SQL dump (if large, use BigDump or host tool)
   - If SSH+Artisan available: php artisan migrate --force (only after backup). **Never run seeders that create demo data in production.**
3. Ensure character set utf8mb4_unicode_ci. Check migrations for SQLite-specific syntax (this repo appears MySQL-friendly).

## 7) Dependencies & caches
If Composer available (CLI): composer install --no-dev --optimize-autoloader
Else upload endor/ from local (repo gitignore currently allows? Check - vendor/ is gitignored in root; do NOT commit vendor unless host absolutely requires it and no other option).

After deploy:
`ash
php artisan config:clear
php artisan cache:clear
php artisan view:clear
php artisan route:clear
# Only if config cached and safe: php artisan config:cache && php artisan route:cache && php artisan view:cache
`
Do not cache routes if routes use closures in some cases; test after caching.

## 8) File permissions
Set writable: storage/ and ootstrap/cache/ to 775 or as host requires. storage/app/public for uploads.

Create symlink for public storage if needed: php artisan storage:link (if SSH available). Otherwise manually symlink or ensure files served from correct path.

## 9) Compatibility notes (important)
- **WebSockets/Reverb/Redis**: If not available, live chat/realtime features may need fallback. The app has Reverb configured; on pure shared hosting without long-running processes, consider polling or disabling realtime only if necessary. Test.
- **Queues**: Use sync or DB driver if no worker/Supervisor/Redis.
- **Node server**: Not required in production if assets prebuilt.
- **Cron**: Only add if features require scheduled tasks and host supports.
- **PHP extensions**: Common needed: BCMath, Ctype, Fileinfo, JSON, Mbstring, OpenSSL, PDO, Tokenizer, XML, GD or Imagick (for images/PDF/barcode).

## 10) Go-live checklist
- [ ] Backups taken
- [ ] .env configured correctly, APP_DEBUG=false
- [ ] DB imported/migrated
- [ ] Document root points to Laravel public/
- [ ] Assets load (CSS/JS)
- [ ] Storage link / uploads accessible
- [ ] Tracking works (guest)
- [ ] Admin login works
- [ ] Photos on shipments display
- [ ] Payment callbacks tested in sandbox/live per gateway
- [ ] Email sending tested
- [ ] No sensitive files exposed
- [ ] Error pages work, no debug output

## 11) Rollback
Restore files from backup and DB dump if issues arise.

